Phase 1: The Ghidra & Java Foundation
Before the MCP could even breathe, we had to ensure the heavy machinery was right.

The Java Requirement: Ghidra 12.x requires JDK 21. We ensured Ubuntu was using the right version since JDK 17 or older causes silent crashes.

The Environment Path: We identified your Ghidra installation at /opt/ghidra_12.0.4_PUBLIC.

Phase 2: Python Environment & MCP Fixes
This was where we spent the most time. We moved from "guessing" the path to "verifying" it.

Finding the "True" Python: We used python3 -c "import sys; print(sys.executable)" to find your actual venv path: /home/ubuntu/Downloads/venv/bin/python3.

Clearing the Lock Files: This was the breakthrough. When Ghidra crashes, it leaves .lock or .lck files that prevent new instances from starting. We ran:

Bash

find /home/ubuntu/Downloads/malware/dcrat/pyghidra_mcp_projects/ -name "*.lck" -delete
The Correct MCP Command: We discovered your version of the MCP server doesn't like the server keyword and instead requires the --transport stdio flag.

Phase 3: The Claude MCP Configuration
We moved away from using the shell env command (which was breaking the communication pipe) and used Claude's native flags.

Bash

claude mcp add pyghidra \
  --env GHIDRA_INSTALL_PATH="/opt/ghidra_12.0.4_PUBLIC" \
  --env _JAVA_OPTIONS="-Djava.awt.headless=true -Xmx2g" \
  -- /home/ubuntu/Downloads/venv/bin/python3 -m pyghidra_mcp --transport stdio
Phase 4: Local Ollama Integration
To stop Claude from asking you to log in to the cloud, we rerouted its brain to your local Qwen 3.5 model.

The Redirect: We set ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL to http://localhost:11434.

The "Fake" Auth: We provided a dummy token so the CLI wouldn't trigger the browser login.

Bash

ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN="ollama" \
ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL="http://localhost:11434" \
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY="local" \
claude --model qwen3.5:9b
